  "response": "Adjudication between an instructional account and an irregularity account depends on evidence that neither the school's administrators nor the outside auditors can reshape after the fact, and the order in which an inquiry unfolds largely determines what such evidence remains available. Because the external audit of the school's testing was set in motion by whistleblower complaints from staff members, that audit brought about the collection of seating charts and test booklet security logs as evidence by the auditors. Records of that sort matter because they fix who was present in each room and who held custody of the secure materials, quite apart from any later characterization of classroom practice.\n\nThe analytic core of such an inquiry is convergence across measures that a genuine instructional improvement would have to move together. Investigators compared the school's standardized test scores with its students' performance on unrelated classroom assessments. Erasure analysis measured the rate of wrong-to-right answer changes on the school's answer sheets, and that measurement produced the finding that the school's wrong-to-right erasure rate exceeded the statewide average by several standard deviations. An excess of that magnitude shows that adults altered student answer sheets after the testing sessions ended. At least one of two breaches of protocol accordingly obtained, and perhaps both of them together: the alteration of answer sheets by adults once the sessions had closed, or students' receipt of advance access to the actual secured test items.\n\nEvidentiary weight is a question distinct from statistical anomaly, because the forum that imposes sanctions applies its own threshold of proof. Erasure analysis is accepted in disciplinary proceedings as conclusive proof of cheating without corroborating evidence. That standing cannot be squared with the administrative law judge's ruling that the erasure evidence alone did not establish misconduct by one named teacher; the sufficiency of unsupported erasure data and the insufficiency found in that teacher's case cannot both hold of the same body of proof.\n\nThe last step of the adjudication is to weigh the trajectory of scores against the explanation offered for it. The school's gains were replicated under a randomized retest administered by independent supervisors, a result that cannot be reconciled with the fact that scores at the school fell sharply in the testing year following the opening of the investigation; a supervised replication rules out so abrupt a decline once scrutiny began. Against that collapse in the following year stands the principal's own account, for she attributed the score gains to a newly introduced intensive tutoring program. Even though she maintained that tutoring explanation throughout, the principal was removed from her position after the inquiry; in other words, once the inquiry had concluded she no longer held the post of principal.",
